Status / Hints / TODO list for MacOS builds

- Requirements:
  - system installs:
    - need full xcode; command line tools is not enough (for qtbase)
      (Xcode includes git and python3.9, but not too much else)
  - Required tools not in MacOS/XCode (or not in a usable version)
    We take these from homebrew, ATM (TODO)
    Some bits have hardcoded paths to /opt/homebrew (TODO).
      - autoconf, automake (only for running autogen.sh)
      - cmake
      - bison (version in macos is too old)
  - Required libs not in the current AppImage build scripts.
    - openjpeg, libzstd, libpng, jpg, libpcre2 -> done (already had Windows builds)
    - pkgconf -> done (had to update package -> TODO: check if Windows build broke)
    - boost -> done (poppler complains about some missing sub-lib; not sure if we
                     should add that)
  - Everything _can_ now be built without external (non-sytem) runtime deps,
    however the build process is still prone to picking up some libs from homebrew
    (hard to rule out, entirely, as long as we are using cmake from homebrew).
    To make sure this did not happen, use src/mac/bundle.py to check for any
    remaining bad dependencies.

- Setup:
  - We need a case sensitive filesystem for building, which is not the default:
    sudo diskutil apfs addVolume disk3 APFSX BuildFS
    (assuming disk3 is the "synthesized" disk as given by `diskutil list`)
    This creates and mounts /Volumes/BuildFS . The important spec is "APFSX".
  - Next point /build and /src to the correct dirs. This can only be done
    via /etc/synthetic.conf . Edit that file to have the following contents
      build Volumes/BuildFS
      src   abs/path/to/gpg4win/repo/
    (no space on the left; the space in the middle _must_ be a single _tab_; note
     the missing initial slashes in all paths)
    - run: sudo /System/Library/Filesystems/apfs.fs/Contents/Resources/apfs.util -t
      or reboot to make /build and /src come up

- Building (in gpg4win repo dir):
  echo "default" > packages/BUILDTYPE     # once
  ./autogen.sh                            # once, and after touching the build system
  ./build.sh --download --mac --builddir=/Some/casesensitive/Volume
  src/mac/bundle.py /build/install /tmp/staging output.dmg

- Running:
  Many things will not work properly until bundled/installed

- Implementation Notes / TODO:
  - We cannot run MacOS as a docker guest, so need a different setup.
    For now we just bypass that (internal --nodocker option), but that's
    just a temporary hack. We probably want to run in a VM.
    https://github.com/s-u/macosvm looks like a good candidate that supports
    MacOS arm64 on arm64, and is geared towards builder scenarios.
  - At a minimum, we should gather non-system build-deps in a ro volume
  - For the time being, several places assume fixed absolute paths for install locations.
    This may actually be in line with common MacOS practices, but prevents a portable
    install, ATM. Affected places:
    - gnupg configure flags: So gnupgconf returns correct paths
      - potential alternative: provide gpgconf.ctl
    - gpgme configure flags: --enable-fixed-path, to allow finding gpg
      - potential alternative: require PATH
    - bundling script sets library locations using abs paths
      - potential alternative: convert to @loader_path references
  - There are some extra patches in patches-mac which we should simply merge
    in the corresponding packages (gpgoljs). This is just to allow
    independent testing.
  - -DCMAKE_MACOSX_BUNDLE=OFF in gpgoljs does not have the desired effect

- Further TODOs:
  - bundling should be complete, but lacks "installer" look
  - entitlements?
  - There is no codesigning at all, yet
  - provide gpgconf.ctl
